<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Gdpr's cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project for the strongest form
    of the consumer-in-Base test there is: Base does not merely *call* this section, it
    *implements* its contract (design §15.5b, Email's IImmediateOutboxProcessor rule).
    IUserDataContributor is the Article 15 fan-out seam that every service owning
    user-scoped tables must implement — ten of them are still in Humans.Application
    (UserService, TeamService, AccountMergeService, RoleAssignmentService, CampService,
    ShiftSignupService, TicketQueryService, AuditLogService and friends) and thirteen are
    already-moved G5 sections. A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Gdpr would therefore
    make Base reference a section and cycle (memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    All five types are here because all five are named from outside:
      - IUserDataContributor + UserDataSlice + GdprExportSections — the contributor half,
        named by 23 implementers across Base and the sections.
      - IGdprExportService + GdprExport — the orchestrator half, named by Shell's
        ProfileController (the /Profile export download) and GuestController.
    Nothing else exists: the section is the 70-line fan-out itself, which is internal.

    References Humans.Interfaces only — the bottom of the graph. IFanout and IOrchestrator
    live there (in the Humans.Base.Interfaces namespace, which is why the two
    interface files carry a using for it).
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Base\Humans.Base.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
